PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The scenario is governed by Ontario's Sale of Goods Act (SGA). Under s. 21, risk passes with property unless the parties agree otherwise. The MacBook was specific goods that required work (screen replacement) to reach a deliverable state, so SGA s. 19 Rule 2 applies: property (and thus risk) does not pass until the seller completes the work *and* gives the buyer notice that the work has been done.

Jake finished the repairs but his June 5 text only stated the laptop was "almost finished" and would be ready the next day; it did not constitute notice that the goods were already in a deliverable state. Property and risk therefore remained with Jake when the flood occurred, obligating him to refund Luke. 

Choice C correctly captures this outcome (repairs completed + notice given, but notice defective). B errs by treating a future readiness date as valid notice. D and E misstate the statutory rules on when property passes. A is irrelevant; the SGA allocation of risk controls regardless of the loss's cause.}

